#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] third and last couple in a family are—

- Masters.
- Servants.

The direction that the apostle gives to servants is set down in this text.

In it he

+ Sets out their duties.
+ Adds motives to perform them.

I. In setting out servants’ duties he notes the #emph[kinds], the #emph[manner], and the #emph[extent] of them.

1\. The kinds of servants’ duties are noted in two words—

- #emph[Obey,] v. 5.
- #emph[Serve,] v. 7.

2\. The manner is set down

- #emph[Affirmatively,] vv. 5, 7.
- #emph[Negatively,] vv. 6, 7.

1\. #emph[Affirmatively,] by showing what graces are needful; for this purpose he lists four particulars.

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ #emph[Fear and trembling.]
+ #emph[Singleness of heart.]
+ #emph[Conscience to Christ.]
+ #emph[Good will.] Under it are included—
  + Cheerfulness.
  + Readiness.
  + Diligence.
  + Faithfulness.
]

2\. #emph[Negatively,] by showing what vices are to be avoided; and these are chiefly two:

1\. #emph[Eye-service,] which is opposed to #emph[doing things—]

- #emph[As Christ’s servants.]
- #emph[From the heart.]

2\. #emph[Men-pleasing,] which is opposed to #emph[doing the will of God.]

3\. The extent of servants’ duties is noted in these four phrases—

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ Masters #emph[according to the flesh,] v. 5.
+ #emph[As to Christ,] v. 5.
+ #emph[As servants of Christ,] v. 6.
+ #emph[Doing God’s will,] v. 6.
]

II. The motives the apostle uses are partly—

- #emph[Implied.]
- #emph[Expressed.]

They are #emph[implied] in three ways.

+ By declaring the place of a master #emph[(as to Christ.)]
+ By pointing out the honor of their service #emph[(as servants of Christ.)]
+ By showing the ground of servants’ subjection #emph[(God’s will.)]

The motive expressed is the #emph[recompense] that servants will have for their pains, fully set out in v. 8.

In it these distinct points are noted in order.

+ The assurance of it #emph[(knowing.)]
+ The ground of it, taken from a general rule #emph[(whatever good thing any man does.)]
+ The particular application of it #emph[(whether slave or free.)]
+ The kind of it #emph[(the same he will receive.)]
+ The author and giver of it #emph[(from the Lord.)]

=== §. 2. #emph[Of the lawfulness of a master’s place and power.]

In handling servants’ duties I will proceed in the order set out above. In the first place, therefore, I will point out the #emph[kinds] of servants’ duties.

To find out the kinds, I will follow the method used in handling children’s duties. Note, then:

+ The #emph[fountain] of servants’ duties.
+ The #emph[streams] that issue from it.

The #emph[fountain] lies partly in the #emph[opinion] and partly in the #emph[feelings] of servants.

In their #emph[opinion] they must be informed and fully persuaded that the #footnote[Servants must be informed of the lawfulness of a master’s authority.] place of a master and of a servant is lawful and warranted: that God in general ordained degrees of superiority and inferiority, of authority and subjection; and in particular gave masters the authority they have, and put servants in the subjection in which they are. Until the judgment is settled on this, neither reverence nor obedience will be given as it ought to be. For reverence has reference to eminence and superiority, and obedience to authority and power. Who will reverence or obey a man whom he takes to be his equal? This was the ground of the conspiracy of Korah, Dathan and Abiram: they thought #emph[Moses and Aaron took too much upon themselves, and lifted themselves up above the congregation of the Lord.]#footnote[Num 16:3.] Therefore I will here note the grounds of a master’s authority and a servant’s subjection. #footnote[Grounds of masters’ and servants’ places.]

1\. #emph[God has given an express commandment] to masters to govern their servants, and to servants to be subject to their masters. #footnote[Exod 20:10.] In the fourth commandment God gives masters a charge over their servants, to see that they do no manner of work. And the angel bids #emph[Hagar humble herself under her mistress’s hands.]#footnote[Gen 16:9.] And here servants are commanded to #emph[obey their masters.]

2\. Many directions are given both to masters and to servants, according to their different places, on how to conduct themselves toward one another. Read for this purpose the many laws that Moses prescribed to both, the many counsels that Solomon also gives to both, in his Proverbs especially, and in particular the directions of this and other apostles.

3\. Saints in all ages have been set in these places, some in the places of masters and some in the places of servants; and according to the place in which God set them, they have performed their duty: masters the duties of masters, and servants the duties of servants.

4\. The many parables Christ uses, taken from the power that masters have and exercise over their servants and the subjection that servants give to their masters, show that the authority of the one and the subjection of the other are things granted without question and not denied.

5\. God has made many promises of reward both to masters and to servants who conscientiously perform the duties of their place, and has made many threats against the one and the other when they are negligent in them.

All these grounds are so clearly and plentifully set down in Scripture that anyone who is at all acquainted with it may know them to be so. Were there no other argument than this text which I have in hand, it would be enough to confound all who speak against them, and to move those who believe all the more #footnote[#emph[Serui cum crediderint plus dominis carnalibus seruire debent. Cypr. Testim. l.] 3. #emph[c.] 72. See Treatise 1, §. 123.] to serve their masters according to the flesh, for the sake of their faith and their profession of it.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of the Anabaptists’ arguments against the authority of masters, and subjection of servants.]

Contrary to this first ground of servants’ subjection is the opinion of the Anabaptists, who teach that all are alike, and that there is no difference between masters and servants. Their reasons, by which they would make a show of proving their unreasonable opinion, are these.

1\. #emph[Objection.] Masters are either unbelievers or Christians, and so are servants, one or the other. If masters are unbelievers and servants Christians, how unfitting it is that Christians should be subject to unbelievers! And if master and servant are both Christians, they are brothers; but brothers are equals, and neither is subject to the other.

#emph[Answer.] Rule and subjection are matters of outward civil order: they tend to the outward preservation of church, commonwealth and family in this world. But faith, piety and such graces are inward matters of the soul, tending to a better life.

Since these are so different, one who is more excellent in the one may be inferior in the other. Indeed, though there is an equality in the one, namely in spiritual things, yet there may be a disparity in the other, namely in social and temporal matters. And though saints may be far inferior to unbelievers in outward condition, yet they are not a bit less glorious before God. The honor that belongs to saints, and is distinctively theirs, is inward, not visible to the carnal eye of a natural man.

2\. #emph[Objection.] It is against nature for one man to be a servant, especially a slave, to another. #footnote[#emph[Prima seruitutis causa peccatum est, etc. Verum & poenalis seruitus ea lege ordinatur, qua naturalem ordinem conseruari iubet, etc. Aug. de Ciu. Dei l.] 19. #emph[c.] 15.]

#emph[Answer.] Even granting that it is against that absolute and perfect nature in which God at first created man, and that it came in by sin, yet it is not against that order and course of nature in which God has now settled man. God has turned many punishments of sin into binding duties, such as #emph[the subjection of the wife to the husband, and man’s eating bread by the sweat of his brow.]

3\. #emph[Objection.] It is the prerogative of Christians to be #emph[all one;]#footnote[Gal 3:28.] but the subjection of servants to masters is against that prerogative.

#emph[Answer.] That prerogative is purely spiritual. For #emph[in Christ all are one] as they are members of Christ, which is a spiritual body, not as they are members of a political body. A political inequality is not against a spiritual equality.

4\. #emph[Objection.] This subjection is against the #footnote[Gal 5:1.] liberty that Christ has purchased for us, with which he has made us free.

#emph[Answer.] It is not. For that liberty is from the curse and rigor of the moral law; from the ceremonial law and its rites; from Satan, sin, death and damnation. But it is not from those degrees which God has established between man and man for the good of mankind.

5\. #emph[Objection.] We are expressly forbidden to be “servants of men.”#footnote[1 Cor 7:23.]

#emph[Answer.] #emph[To be a servant] in that place is not merely to be in subjection #footnote[See Treatise 1, §. 124, 125, 126.] under another and to do service for him, but to be so obsequious to a man, so bent on pleasing him, and so subject to his will, as to do whatever he wants done: to regard nothing but his pleasure, and to prefer it before God’s word and will. It is not, therefore, the thing itself, but an excess in it, that is forbidden there.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of a servant’s fear of his master.]

The other part of that fountain from which the duties #footnote[Servants ought to fear their masters.] of servants flow lies in the feelings; and it is, in one word, #emph[fear,] which is an awe-filled dread of a master: an #emph[awe] because of his master’s place, a #emph[dread] because of his master’s power. #emph[Awe] is such a reverent esteem of his master as makes him count his master “worthy of all honor,” which Saint Paul#footnote[1 Tim 6:1.] expressly enjoins servants to do. #emph[Dread] is such a fear of provoking his master’s wrath as makes him think over and consider every way to please him. This is what the apostle implies here under these two words, #emph[fear] and #emph[trembling.] In both these respects Saint Peter commands servants to be subject “with all fear.” #footnote[1 Pet 2:18.]

This #emph[fear] is so much a servant’s own, in relation to his master, that where it is lacking there is a plain denial of his master’s place and power. God implies this in this reproachful question: “If I am a master, where is your#super[+] fear of Me?”#footnote[Mal 1:6.] That is, you#super[+] plainly show that you#super[+] do not count me your#super[+] master, because in your#super[+] heart there is no #emph[fear] of me.

This fear will draw servants on to perform every duty cheerfully. The more it abounds, the more desire and striving there will be to please and to give full satisfaction (and this is a point commanded to servants, “to please them well in all things”). #footnote[Titus 2:9.] Indeed, it will gladden a servant’s heart to see his service prosper. We have a worthy pattern of this in Abraham’s servant, #footnote[Gen 24.] whose care to do his business as his master would have it, and his prayer for God’s help in it, and his thanks for God’s blessing on it, show the awe-filled respect he bore his master.

Again, on the other side, this fear will keep men from offending their masters (which was one reason that moved Joseph not to yield to his mistress)#footnote[Gen 39:8.] and in this respect it may prevent many harms that their masters’ offense and wrath might bring on them.

One special means to breed and preserve this fear in servants is a due consideration of the ground of their masters’ #footnote[Direction.] place and power, which is God’s appointment. God has placed them in his stead, and in part given them his power: they are God’s deputies and ministers, and therefore in Scripture the title#footnote[אדון Gen 24:9. Κύριος. Eph 6:5.] (#emph[Lord]) is given to them in a distinctive way. What makes subjects stand in awe of lower magistrates? Is it not because they represent the king’s person, and have authority and power given to them by the king?

=== §. 5. #emph[Of the extremes contrary to servants’ fear of their masters.]

Two extremes are contrary to this servant-like fear.

1\. In the excess, a #emph[slavish fear:] when they fear nothing but #footnote[Slavish fear in servants.] the avenging power of their master, the staff or the cudgel, as we say; so long as they may avoid that, they do not care whether their master is pleased or not. This often makes them wish that their masters had no power over them, or that they were dead. This was the fear that possessed the heart of that unprofitable servant who said to his master, “I knew that #footnote[Matt 25:24, 25.] you are a hard man … so I was afraid.” Such servile servants will never be profitable.

2\. In the defect, a slight esteem and plain contempt of #footnote[Servants despising their masters. Mal 1:6.] masters. That this is contrary to fear is evident from the opposition God makes between them in these words: “If I am a master, where is your#super[+] fear of Me, O you#super[+] who despise My name?” As if he had said: by this you#super[+] show that you#super[+] do not fear me as a master, because you#super[+] despise me. This sin of despising masters is expressly #footnote[1 Tim 6:2.] forbidden, and for it #emph[Hagar was harshly dealt with.]#footnote[Gen 16:6.] When masters are poor, lowly, weak, aged or otherwise feeble, proud servants are prone to despise them. This shows a base mind, and proves that they respect their master’s power more than his place. The poorest and weakest have the same place and authority over servants that the richest and strongest have; all bear God’s image alike. But disdainful, proud servants show that they do not regard God’s image at all.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of servants’ reverence in speech.]

The two main #emph[streams] that issue from the #emph[fountain] named above are—

- #emph[Reverence.]
- #emph[Obedience.]

#emph[Reverence] is shown in

- #emph[Speech.]
- #emph[Bearing.]

In speech, by #emph[refraining from] speech and by #emph[ordering] it #emph[well.]

Servants’ reverence in refraining from speech is shown in three ways.

1\. By holding back from speaking without just cause in their master’s presence or hearing. This phrase#footnote[1 Kgs 10:8.] (“who stand continually before you and hear your wisdom”), spoken of Solomon’s servants, shows that they were slow to speak and quick to listen in their master’s presence.

2\. By refraining from replying when they observe that their master is unwilling for them to speak any more. Peter refrained in this way when his master gave him this short answer: “What is that to you?”#footnote[John 21:22.]

3\. By attending to what their masters say to them. For servants ought to show such respect to their masters when they speak to them as Samuel did to God when he said, “Speak, for Your servant is listening.”#footnote[1 Sam 3:10.] The titles of #emph[Lord] and #emph[servant] show that this speech is taken from the duty of servants. The derivation of the #footnote[ὑπακούετε. See Treatise 1, §. 96, 124.] Greek word the apostle uses, translated #emph[obey,] implies as much. Abraham’s #footnote[Gen 24:9.] servant showed this reverence to his master when he gave him a charge about choosing a wife for his son.

For ordering their speech well to their masters, servants must observe five cautions.

1\. That they have just occasion to speak: that is, either when their masters require them to speak (as #footnote[Matt 16:14.] the disciples did), or when they see that it is needful for their masters that they should speak. In such cases #emph[speech] shows reverence, as well as #emph[silence] does in other cases. The general points delivered before about the reverence of #footnote[Treatise 3, §. 13.] wives to their husbands, and of #footnote[Treatise 5, §. 4. 5. #emph[etc.]] children to their parents, may be applied to servants, and pressed on them by an argument from the less to the greater, like this: if wives (who in many things share a joint authority with their husbands) and children (who are not subject to their parents in so servile a degree as servants are to their masters) must show their inward fear of their husbands and parents by outward reverence, much more must servants show it to their masters. To show the force of this inference all the more, let it be noted that the apostle adds here another kind #footnote[When it is needful for servants to speak to masters.] of word than he did before in either wives’ or children’s duties, namely #footnote[See Treatise 1, §. 124.] #emph[trembling.]

#emph[Question.] In what cases may it be needful for masters that their servants speak to them?

#emph[Answer.] 1. When servants know anything that may be profitable for their masters, they ought to make it known, though they are not asked; as #footnote[2 Kgs 5:3.] that maid did who told her mistress, Naaman’s wife, that there was a prophet in Samaria who could cure him of his leprosy.

2\. When masters, through ignorance, anger or some such cause, refuse to do what their servants know to be good for them, the servants ought to persuade their masters to do it; as Naaman’s servants persuaded their master to wash himself in #footnote[2 Kgs 5:13.] the Jordan, as the prophet advised him.

3\. When servants are not fully instructed in the things they ought to do for their master, they ought to ask him what is to be done, as the disciples did, who said to their master, “Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the #footnote[Matt 26:17.] Passover?”

4\. When some scruple arises in their minds about any charge their master gives them, they may inquire of their master, as Abraham’s servant did when he said, “What if the woman #footnote[Gen 24:5.] is unwilling to follow me …?”

5\. When their master unjustly suspects them of any evil, they may speak to clear their own innocence, as David #footnote[1 Sam 24:10.] did to Saul.

A second caution for servants’ ordering their speech well is #footnote[Directions for servants’ framing their speech well to masters.] that the very form and manner of their speech, when they have just occasion to speak to their masters, should savor of reverence. For this purpose let these particulars be noted.

1\. That servants give respectful titles to their masters.

It was out of honorable respect for their master that Naaman’s servants called him #emph[father.]#footnote[2 Kgs 5:13.] In Scripture #footnote[§. 4.] one of God’s titles is given to masters, namely #emph[Lord.]

2\. That they use few words, no more than are needed, even when they have occasion to speak; as may be gathered from the examples cited above to show when servants might and ought to speak.

3\. That all the words they speak to their master should be meek, mild and humble. Note for this purpose how the sons of the prophets framed their speech to their master. #footnote[2 Kgs 6:2. #emph[etc.]]

1\. They humbly ask leave for themselves to go to the Jordan.

2\. They entreat him to go, in these words: “Please come with your servants.”

3\. When one of them had lost the head of his axe, as if he did not dare be so bold as to speak to his master to get it back for him, he refers the matter to his good pleasure, saying, “Oh, my master, it was borrowed!”

A third caution is that they observe a fit time to speak to their master: as when he is at leisure to hear them (thus it is noted of the disciples that when their master was #emph[alone] they put their questions to him)#footnote[Mark 4:10.] or when his mind is quiet, not troubled with anger (thus when David observed that Saul’s mind was somewhat calmed toward him, by the words he used, “Is that your voice, David my son?” he then took occasion to speak to him more freely). #footnote[1 Sam 26:17.]

A fourth caution is that they give a ready and prompt answer to their master. This is often commended in Peter: when Christ put any question to his disciples, he would answer at once and #footnote[Matt 16:16. John 6:68.] readily.

A fifth caution is that all their speeches and answers to their master should be true, not daring to tell them a lie. This shows a very reverent respect for a master. When Eli asked Samuel what the Lord had said to him, “Samuel told him everything and did not hide a thing from him.”#footnote[1 Sam 3:18. 2 Sam 14:18, 19.] And when David bade the woman of Tekoa not to hide from him the thing he would ask her, she told him the whole truth. There is a double bond to tie servants to this:

+ Their conscience toward God, who is a God of truth.
+ Their respect for their masters, with whom they ought to deal faithfully.

Lastly, a servant’s reverence ought to be shown by his speech about his master even behind his back, speaking good of him and no evil. Abraham’s servant also showed this part of reverence to his master.#footnote[Gen 24:35, #emph[etc.]] In this way they will show themselves good, true-hearted, faithful servants, and not flatterers.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of the vices contrary to a servant’s reverence in speech.]

The offenses contrary to the #emph[reverence] of servants to their masters, named above, are these.

1\. #emph[Sauciness] and boldness in servants, when they have no more respect for their master’s presence than for anyone else’s, but are full of chatter and loud in speech before their master, or in the room next to him, where they may be heard as well as if they were in the same room. Masters are often much offended in this way.

2\. #emph[Insistence] in speaking, and replying again and again, though their masters not only show their dislike of it but also expressly charge them to speak no more. Scolding maids who will have the last word over their mistress offend much in this. This goes directly against the apostle’s prohibition to servants, “not answering back.” #footnote[Titus 2:9.]

3\. #emph[Impatience,] when they cannot bear to hear their master finish what he is saying, but either interrupt him or flounce off.

4\. #emph[Defiance,] when (as Solomon notes) “though they understand, #footnote[Prov 29:19.] they will not speak.” Though they know very well that it is their master’s pleasure that they should speak, indeed though they are bidden to speak, yet their stubborn pride and sullen heart will not allow them to speak, no, not even though it is most needful for their masters. If they know that their master is going on in a course very harmful to him, yet they will not tell him of it. Indeed, if their master unjustly suspects them of any evil, they will let him remain in that suspicion rather than speak to clear themselves. And if their master does not expressly appoint them from time to time what to do, they will never ask; nor, if they are in doubt about what he gives them in charge, will they inquire further to be satisfied about it. This kind of silence, in these and similar cases, shows more doggedness than dutifulness.

5\. #emph[Disdain,] when they #footnote[See §. 5.] scorn to give the title #emph[master] to him who is set over them, because he is a poor and lowly man.

6\. #emph[Arrogance,] when their words are high and lofty against their master, claiming that they are as good as he, though for a time they are under him. Clerks, apprentices, waiting women and the like, born of gentlemen and men of good rank, are for the most part guilty of this fault. The reason is that their birth and parentage make them forget their present place and condition; or else (which is worse) make them willfully set themselves above it.

7\. #emph[Muttering] and grumbling on every occasion of discontent, by which it comes about that they often provoke much wrath (for “a harsh word stirs up anger”). #footnote[Prov 15:1.]

8\. #emph[Ill-timed interruption] of their master: speaking to him when he is seriously occupied in some weighty business (as those did who told Christ of his mother and brothers#footnote[Mark 3:32.] when he was preaching), or speaking to him at an unfit time while he is angry, by which they often bring much harm on themselves.

9\. #emph[Answering their master at their own leisure,] letting him call, and call again and again. Job complains of this, saying, “I call for my servant, but he does not answer.” #footnote[Job 19:16.]

10\. #emph[Slapping their master in the mouth with a lie,] like Gehazi, who, when he had wickedly got money and clothing from Naaman, and his master asked him where he had been, said with a straight face, “Your servant did not go anywhere.”#footnote[2 Kgs 5:25.] Let the judgment carried out on him make all servants beware of the like sin. For #emph[lying] is in itself a heinous sin, yet all the more heinous when it is told to one who has authority over us and, because of that authority, stands in God’s place.

Lastly, #emph[evil talk about their master behind his back.] This is a sin even though what is spoken to a master’s disgrace is true; for a master’s infirmities ought rather to be covered than revealed and laid open by a servant. How monstrous a sin it is, then, to raise slanderous reports against a master that are untrue! This was Ziba’s sin against Mephibosheth his master. #footnote[2 Sam 16:3.]

So much for servants’ reverence in speech to their masters.

Their reverence in bearing follows.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of servants’ reverent behavior to their masters.]

To show a servant’s #emph[reverence in bearing] toward his master, three things are especially required.

+ Dutiful gestures of respect.
+ Respectful behavior.
+ Modest clothing.

I. Such dutiful and submissive gestures of respect and courtesy as befit their sex and place, and that according to the most usual #footnote[1\. Servants’ gestures of respect to their masters.] custom of the country and place where they are, servants must perform to their masters, as they have occasion to go to them, to come from them, to receive any charge from them, or to bring any message to them. Where Isaac says, in the blessing he gave Jacob, “May you be the master of your brothers, and may the sons of your mother bow #footnote[Gen 27:29.] down to you,” by that phrase, #emph[bowing down,] he notes the condition of a servant, and with it implies a servant’s duty. On this ground, when the sons of the prophets saw that the spirit of Elijah rested on Elisha, taking it for a clear sign that God had made him a governor and master over them, “they went to #footnote[2 Kgs 2:15.] meet him and bowed down to the ground before him.”

II. Corresponding to a servant’s gestures of respect must be his whole behavior #footnote[2\. Servants’ dutiful behavior before their masters.] before his master, seasoned and ordered with such modesty and humility as may show an honorable respect for his master; as:

1\. To #emph[stand] in his master’s presence, which testifies a readiness to perform any service his master appoints him to do. This was one thing noted and commended by the queen of Sheba in Solomon’s servants: she saw their #emph[standing,]#footnote[1 Kgs 10:5, 8.] and said, “How blessed are these servants of yours who stand … before you!” Where it is said that she also saw their #emph[sitting,] that phrase shows a seemly order they kept even when they were out of his presence, by giving and taking their right and due place.

2\. By uncovering their heads in their master’s presence: in our days, and in the parts of the world where we live, this is for males a sign and token of subjection.

3\. By sobriety and modesty both in expression and in the whole bearing of the body, especially when servants are in their master’s presence. For to compose the expression and the whole body soberly because of someone’s presence shows a reverent respect for that person, for whose sake that sobriety is shown.

III. The clothing servants wear must also be so fashioned #footnote[3\. Servants’ seemly clothing.] and ordered as to declare them to be servants and under their masters; and so it will show a reverent respect for their masters. One purpose of clothing is to show a difference between superiors and inferiors, persons in authority and those under subjection. It pleased the Holy Spirit to note this particular #emph[(and their] #footnote[1 Kgs 10:5.] #emph[attire)] as a point worth observing in Solomon’s servants.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of the faults of servants contrary to reverence in bearing.]

The bearing of many servants toward their master is wholly contrary to the reverence named above:

1\. Some, through rudeness and lack of good upbringing, #footnote[1\. Rudeness.] come to their masters and go from them as to and from their fellows: no sign of reverence by any gesture of respect; they do not know how to give it. By this they dishonor their parents and the way they were brought up.

2\. Others, if on first coming to their master they give some #footnote[2\. Too much familiarity.] greeting, yet, through too much familiarity with their masters, all the rest of the day they will behave as if they were his fellows, scarcely uncovering their heads in their master’s presence, not bearing to stand long before him, but either sitting down or slinking away when they should be in attendance. No doubt this proverb arose from this excessive familiarity: “Good morning for all day.” This unmannerly familiarity is commonly found in servants who have poor and lowly masters. For the servants of rich and great men can be so full of courtesy that not a word will be spoken by their masters to them, or by them to their masters, without the knee being bowed with it. They can stand hour after hour before their masters and not once put on their hats. If they are walking behind their masters, their master will not turn round sooner than their hats will be off, and that as often as he turns or speaks to them. Why should rich masters have so much reverence shown to them, and poor masters none at all? Do not the poor bear God’s image as well as the rich? Does God’s word make any difference between rich and poor? Does it say, “Serve rich masters with fear and trembling”? Surely it is the corruption of man’s heart that makes this difference. Servants by nature regard their master’s outward ability more than his inward authority, and the honor that the world confers on men more than the honor that God confers. For God has given as much honor and authority to a poor master as to a rich one, because he is a master.

Servants commonly fail most in this duty of reverence toward their masters in the very place where they should most of all show it, namely in the courts of God and the assembly of God’s people, where the very angels are present to behold our seemly conduct. In this way they cause the ministry of the word to be spoken evil of, and so make their sin all the more heinous.

3\. Exceedingly great is the fault of servants in their excess in clothing. #footnote[3\. Pride.] Ordinarily there is no distinction between a man’s children and his servants; indeed, none between masters and their men, mistresses and their maids. While men and maids are provided for at their masters’ and mistresses’ expense, a difference may perhaps be made; though even then, if they can get the means in any way, they will do what they can to be as finely dressed as they can. But once they provide for themselves, they will lay out all they have on clothing rather than not be as fine as master or mistress: if not so costly, yet in show as showy and fine. New fashions are taken up as soon by servants as by masters and mistresses. What is the end of this, but to be thought as good as master or mistress? If the queen of Sheba were living now, she would wonder as much at the disorder of servants in these days as she then wondered at the seemly order of Solomon’s servants. Let these proud servants look to it: for if God has threatened to “punish … the king’s sons … who are dressed in strange apparel,” #footnote[Zeph 1:8.] can servants who are so dressed expect to go scot-free?

So much for servants’ reverence. Their obedience follows.
